WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  free at last it's a sentiment shared by  millions of chileans after 18 months of  a state of emergency imposed by the  government because of the pandemic  a non-interrupted curfew one of the  world's longest forced people to rush  home early every day now chileans are  hoping to get back to a semi-normal life  [Music]  discotheques and bars will also finally  be able to reopen until the wee hours of  the morning  we're very happy this is good for the  economy and for people's state of mind  it's been too long  it was about time now we can finally go  out at night and have a social life that  we really needed  so far there have been more than 3 700  coronavirus related deaths in chile and  some 1.6 million confirmed cases  health officials and politicians concede  that the long-running curfew had become  unnecessary but truth be told it was  becoming almost impossible to enforce  yet not everyone is happy about the  restrictions being lifted  my friends are going to want to kill me  but i wish we still had the curfew i  could tell guests that it was time to  leave or i could leave early by just  saying oops it's almost curfew time to  go  although there's no longer a curfew  plenty of restrictions still remain  19 infections have dropped dramatically  in chile more than 90 percent of the  target population has been fully  vaccinated and some people have even  received a third dose but as you can see  you still have to wear a face mask  everywhere except while eating and you  also have to show proof of vaccination  to enter most places  chile's borders are now also finally  open to international travelers a  lifesaver for the crippled tourist  industry but anyone who enters the  country must go into quarantine for five  to seven days  it's a glass at least half full  unfortunately these newfound freedoms  could disappear if infection rates begin  to surge once again  lucia newman al jazeera santiago

